      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Debates about green growth relate to high-income countries. Lower-income countries typically have much lower emissions per capita, which makes the mitigation and decoupling rates required for them to stay within their fair-share carbon budgets more modest and therefore more achievable."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "With adequate access to the necessary finance and technology, freedom to use industrial policy, and a development strategy focused on human needs, lower-income countries should be able to stay within their fair-share carbon budgets even while increasing production and consumption to achieve decent living standards for all."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "It is worth noting that virtually all dominant climate-mitigation scenarios involve continued economic growth in high-income countries. The problems created by this approach are concealed in these scenarios by relying on unrealistic assumptions about decoupling and energy efficiency, unrealistic assumptions about the rollout rate of renewable energy, unrealistic and risky assumptions about future negative-emission technologies, highly unequal international burden sharing (future cumulative emissions per capita), and undermining energy use and development in low-income and middle-income countries."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Post-growth approaches would enable societies to largely avoid these problems, thus improving technological feasibility as well as international and intergenerational equity."
